ow fast do we  want to go to some position and how to  stop ourselves from flying around  unintentionally this is true both in the  sim and in reality in the sim we can  probably have more force than would ever  be even possible via these servo motors  but even in real life if you move too  quickly you could fall over and so on so  i'll start by creating a new file called  jump dot pi to try to solve this  to begin i think the way to do this is  to just have a starting vector wherever  we are and then we have a target vector  where we want to be then we'll have a  variable to determine how fast we can  actually get there which will just be an  increment per step in this case again  i'm sure there's a better ways to do  this this is just what i've thought of  for now so then i'll iterate through the  current position and target positions  per each unit in the position vectors  i'll calculate the difference between  these then i'm going to want to modify  the position vector which will later  feed to the simulator as i iterate over  the current position vector so i'll make  a deep copy of the current vector i'll  also be using the index value of the  positions to make edits so i'll add in  an enumerate around the zip and make  c-paws and t-paws a tuple i wasn't  actually sure this syntax was going to  be valid i don't think i've ever done  that before so i tested it to confirm  and it still worked that's pretty cool  if the absolute value of the difference  is greater than the increment then we  want this position to move only by the  sign of the increment and not the full  amount otherwise if it's not too large  then we can move by the full target  difference let's loop through this 5  times to start and don't forget to save  to the new vector at the end and load  the current vector every time using this  for control might very well turn out to  be too sloppy slow and unreliably timed  for an actual robot or even the  simulated one for control and i  acknowledge that