ng that position file  which is asking for a stand so biddle  goes as fast as possible to the new  position and this causes the jump  i still want to test the new positions  that i came up with and at least this  time it doesn't flip but ironically  enough the accidental jump is actually  better than what i've just come up with  so i know that the accidental jump  started off with all joints at zero  which is this sort of kneeling position  so i'll go ahead and just set up a  vector with those values i test this  just the kneel to make sure that it  works and that looks good to me  so then i add in a move to the tall  position at a fast pace essentially as  fast as possible and look at that it  worked  let's try that one more time  beautiful  and with that i think we're at a good  pausing point the next objective for me  is going to be to walk or do some other  complex method  but i don't want to do this manually i  want to use machine learning to at least  walk or do something else in the  simulator and if i can figure that out  then i want to attempt to use the same  machine learning model in the actual  biddle robot to do this we have quite a  few hurdles to overcome but i have a few  ideas on how i want to do that and we'll  see how that goes i'm sure it'll be  super smooth if you are interested in  machine learning and specifically deep  learning then check out the neural  networks from scratch book by myself and  daniel kukewa there we break down all of  the concepts of dense neural networks  into massive detail writing essentially  an entire deep learning framework from  scratch in python so as to get an  understanding of how neural networks  actually work if you're looking to  understand things with deep learning  past just simply calling functions this  is definitely a book for you you can buy  the ebook softcover or hardcover book at  nnfs.io and the softcover and hardcover  come with the ebook version thanks for  watching everyone and i'll see you in  ano